<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Æthelflæd</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 870–<a href="/years/918">918</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Æthelflæd, Lady of the Mercians, Lady of the Mercians</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Æthelflæd, Lady of the Mercians, was the eldest daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-the-great">Alfred the Great</a> and ruled English Mercia from the death of her husband Æthelred in 911 until her own death in 918. Working with her brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-elder">Edward the Elder</a>, she built fortified burhs and led campaigns that recovered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derby">Derby</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leicester">Leicester</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a>. In 916 her forces attacked the Welsh royal crannog at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llangorse-lake">Llangorse Lake</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aethelflaed.jpg" alt="Æthelflæd" width="189" height="284" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AnonymousUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%C3%86thelfl%C3%A6d_as_depicted_in_the_cartulary_of_Abingdon_Abbey.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
